INIT.CONF(5)                File Formats                  INIT.CONF(5)

NAME
    init.conf - programs started and supervised by init

SYNOPSIS
    /etc/init.conf

DESCRIPTION
    init reads the file at boot and processes the entries in order. A
    line holds words separated by blanks; # starts a comment. The first
    word is the keyword, the second the entry name, then options of the
    form key=value, then the command and its arguments. Commands are
    looked up in PATH.

    env NAME=VALUE...
        set variables in the environment of every program started
        afterwards.

    task NAME [options] COMMAND...
        run the command and wait for it before the next entry.

    service NAME [options] COMMAND...
        run the command in the background. When it exits it is started
        again, after a second if it ran less than five seconds; after
        five such quick exits in a row init gives up and leaves the
        entry failed until initctl starts it.

    console NAME [options] COMMAND...
        the session on the console: the command gets its own process
        group and the terminal, and is always restarted when it ends.
        sh gives a shell, startgui a graphical session.

OPTIONS
    if=PATH          start only when PATH exists (skipped otherwise)
    log=FILE         append standard output and error to FILE
    restart=always   restart a service whenever it exits (default)
    restart=never    a service that exits stays stopped
    restart=failure  restart only after a non zero status or a signal

    Without a readable file init uses a built-in table equivalent to
    the example below, so the system still boots to a login prompt.

EXAMPLE
    env PATH=/bin TERM=minios
    task fsinit fsinit
    service audio if=/dev/pcm0 audiod
    task network net apply
    service dhcp restart=failure dhcpc -a
    console login login

SEE ALSO
    initctl(1), fsinit(1), login(1), fstab(5)
